---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CaseStudyCard from '../components/CaseStudyCard.astro';
import { site, conversationMailto } from '../data/site.ts';
import { yearsOfExperience } from '../data/experience.ts';

const studies = (await getCollection('caseStudies', ({ data }) => !data.draft)).sort(
  (a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order,
);

const notes = (await getCollection('notes', ({ data }) => !data.draft)).sort(
  (a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order,
);

const years = yearsOfExperience();
---

<Base title={`${site.name} — ${site.role}`} description={site.description} bareTitle>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-wide px-5 pt-20 pb-16 sm:px-8 sm:pt-28 sm:pb-20">
    <p class="eyebrow">{site.role} · Systems that hold money and time · Remote</p>

    <h1 class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-4xl leading-[1.1] sm:text-5xl lg:text-6xl">
      I build production systems where AI agents write most of the code.
    </h1>

    <div class="mt-8 max-w-2xl space-y-5 text-lg text-muted">
      <p>
        {years} years of backend engineering. Most of it agency work on e-commerce platforms — high-traffic
        stores processing transactions and customer data, including Dyson’s. The kind of systems where
        an outage means lost orders rather than a failed build.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then I stopped, and spent six years travelling. I came back to engineering in March
        2026, started building with coding agents, and the two production systems written up
        below are what came of it — not as a portfolio list, but as the decisions, trade-offs
        and mistakes that actually made them.
      </p>
      <p>
        More than 8 in 10 commits across those systems are agent-written — counted from git
        history, not estimated — inside verification gates built so the output can be trusted
        without depending on my attention.{' '}
        <a href="/work/agent-operated-codebase/" class="link-underline text-accent">
          How that works
        </a> is stated plainly rather than left to be inferred.
      </p>
      <p>
        Building that way moved the interesting questions. Not whether agents can write code —
        they write most of mine — but what that actually does to delivery, quality and cost,
        what should stay human, and how you would prove any of it to a skeptic. I am measuring
        my own systems to find out, publishing the numbers as they land, and talking to teams
        doing the same.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <a href="/work/agent-operated-codebase/" class="btn btn-primary">Read the case study</a>
      <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-secondary">Talk to me about agents in your team</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section
    aria-labelledby="evidence-heading"
    class="border-y border-line bg-band py-16 sm:py-20"
  >
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-wide px-5 sm:px-8">
      <p class="eyebrow">The evidence</p>
      <h2 id="evidence-heading" class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-3xl">
        Claims about AI-native engineering are cheap. These are counted.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-muted">
        Everything this site argues rests on artifacts you can inspect: figures counted from git
        history by script, a practice written up with its failures included, and a tool you can
        run yourself. Where a number is an estimate, it says so.
      </p>

      <dl class="mt-10 grid gap-x-10 gap-y-6 sm:grid-cols-3">
        <div>
          <dt class="eyebrow">The practice</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1.5 text-muted">
            Two production systems, more than 8 in 10 commits agent-written, run solo inside
            verification gates —{' '}
            <a href="/work/agent-operated-codebase/" class="link-underline">the case study</a>,
            including where it rotted.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="eyebrow">The tool</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1.5 text-muted">
            groundtruth — an open-source CLI and GitHub Action that fails CI when an agent
            instruction goes false. Born from{' '}
            <a href="/audit/" class="link-underline">auditing my own context layer</a>; it gates
            this site’s own pull requests.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="eyebrow">The measurements</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1.5 text-muted">
            Delivery metrics from 5 months of agent-operated production history — authorship,
            rework, cycle time, cost — publishing to{' '}
            <a href="/notes/" class="link-underline">the notes</a> as they land.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <p class="mt-10 max-w-2xl text-muted">
        One operator’s systems are a starting point, not an answer. The point of measuring mine
        honestly is to have something worth comparing with yours.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section
    aria-labelledby="work-heading"
    class="mx-auto max-w-wide px-5 py-16 sm:px-8 sm:py-20"
  >
    <h2 id="work-heading" class="text-3xl">Selected work</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-muted">
      The agent-operated practice this work runs on, and the two products it ships — one in
      production carrying real payments, one running and pre-launch. All described with their
      limitations intact.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
      {studies.map((entry) => <CaseStudyCard entry={entry} />)}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="notes-heading" class="border-y border-line bg-band py-16 sm:py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-wide px-5 sm:px-8">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-end justify-between gap-4">
        <div>
          <h2 id="notes-heading" class="text-3xl">Notes</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 max-w-xl text-muted">Shorter pieces on specific problems.</p>
        </div>
        <a href="/notes/" class="link-underline text-sm text-accent">All notes →</a>
      </div>

      <ul class="mt-10 divide-y divide-line border-y border-line">
        {
          notes.map((note) => (
            <li class="group relative flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-4 gap-y-1 py-5">
              <span class="w-28 shrink-0 font-mono text-xs text-muted">{note.data.topic}</span>
              <h3 class="text-lg">
                <a
                  href={`/notes/${note.id}/`}
                  class="after:absolute after:inset-0 after:content-[''] group-hover:text-accent"
                >
                  {note.data.title}
                </a>
              </h3>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section
    aria-labelledby="contact-heading"
    class="mx-auto max-w-wide px-5 py-16 sm:px-8 sm:py-20"
  >
    <h2 id="contact-heading" class="max-w-2xl text-3xl">
      If your team builds with agents, I want to hear how it is really going.
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-muted">
      I am researching how software engineering changes inside real teams as agents write more
      of the code — the parts that work and the parts that do not. Twenty minutes, nothing sold,
      notes compared both ways. Two or three sentences about your team and how agents are used
      is enough to start.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <a href={conversationMailto} class="btn btn-primary">{site.email}</a>
      <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-secondary">Other ways to reach me</a>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-10 max-w-2xl text-sm text-muted">
      I am also open to backend, platform or founding engineering roles — most useful where the
      hard part is the system rather than the screen.{' '}
      <a href="/contact/" class="link-underline">That conversation starts here</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
